1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,610
Your next favourite skincare ingredient may
have been invented by bacteria

2
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:07,087
trying not to die. Not
snail mucin. Not salmon DNA.

3
00:00:07,087 --> 00:00:09,547
And not another form
of hyaluronic acid.

4
00:00:09,797 --> 00:00:14,827
This tiny molecule helps microorganisms survive
places so salty and so

5
00:00:14,827 --> 00:00:18,667
dry that an ordinary cell would
lose its water and stop functioning.

6
00:00:18,817 --> 00:00:20,727
And it is now turning up,

7
00:00:20,727 --> 00:00:24,017
quietly, in Korean serums,
creams and sunscreens.

8
00:00:24,017 --> 00:00:27,735
It is called ectoin, and the real
science is stranger than the marketing.

9
00:00:27,965 --> 00:00:32,537
Ectoin is what researchers
call an extremolyte.

10
00:00:32,617 --> 00:00:34,074
That sounds like a superhero.

11
00:00:34,178 --> 00:00:38,498
It just means a protective molecule
made by microbes living somewhere extreme.

12
00:00:38,748 --> 00:00:41,611
The bacterium we get most of
it from has a name.

13
00:00:41,611 --> 00:00:44,224
Halomonas elongata. It lives in salt.

14
00:00:44,474 --> 00:00:46,420
When the salt outside gets punishing,

15
00:00:46,420 --> 00:00:47,826
it floods itself with ectoin.

16
00:00:48,076 --> 00:00:49,076
Why?

17
00:00:49,096 --> 00:00:51,084
Because salt pulls water
out of cells.

18
00:00:51,084 --> 00:00:53,121
Lose enough water and proteins
start to lose their shape,

19
00:00:53,408 --> 00:00:56,896
membranes get unstable, and
the cell stops working.

20
00:00:57,066 --> 00:01:00,682
keeps the space around
those fragile structures survivable.

21
00:01:00,682 --> 00:01:03,282
Which led skincare researchers
to an obvious question.

22
00:01:03,302 --> 00:01:05,894
Could it do the same
for stressed human skin?

23
00:01:06,434 --> 00:01:10,621
Here is where ectoin stops
behaving like a normal moisturiser.

24
00:01:10,621 --> 00:01:12,714
A familiar humectant, like glycerin,

25
00:01:12,904 --> 00:01:16,178
or hyaluronic acid, attracts water
and holds onto onto it.

26
00:01:16,178 --> 00:01:20,005
Ectoin seems to work on how
water arranges itself around proteins

27
00:01:20,005 --> 00:01:21,657
and membranes.

28
00:01:21,907 --> 00:01:25,538
Scientists call the idea preferential
exclusion. The tidy version.

29
00:01:25,669 --> 00:01:28,139
Ectoin protects proteins by
staying off their surface,

30
00:01:28,139 --> 00:01:30,968
which helps the water
around them stay organised.

31
00:01:31,043 --> 00:01:32,535
The honest version is messier.

32
00:01:32,535 --> 00:01:36,439
In the simulations, ectoin also showed
some attraction to that surface.

33
00:01:36,439 --> 00:01:39,666
And how far it stays back
depends on how tightly the

34
00:01:39,666 --> 00:01:43,084
protein's own water is already arranged.

35
00:01:43,334 --> 00:01:46,308
Think of a celebrity inside
a ring of security.

36
00:01:46,308 --> 00:01:47,743
Ectoin is not hugging the celebrity.

37
00:01:47,799 --> 00:01:50,317
It hangs back, and helps
the ring hold its shape.

38
00:01:50,705 --> 00:01:54,990
Which is why calling it another
hydrating ingredient misses the interesting part.

39
00:01:55,540 --> 00:01:58,621
Your outer skin barrier
is under constant load.

40
00:01:58,860 --> 00:02:03,426
Dry air, cleansing, pollution,
sun, strong actives.

41
00:02:03,426 --> 00:02:06,450
When it struggles, water
escapes more easily,

42
00:02:06,450 --> 00:02:08,101
and skin can feel tight,

43
00:02:08,101 --> 00:02:10,294
rough, or unusually reactive.

44
00:02:10,544 --> 00:02:15,194
Lab research suggests ectoin can help
stabilise biological structures and improve

45
00:02:15,444 --> 00:02:17,094
hydration in that outer layer.

46
00:02:17,094 --> 00:02:19,444
One study found it
changed how keratin,

47
00:02:19,444 --> 00:02:21,804
the main protein in
our outer skin cells,

48
00:02:21,934 --> 00:02:23,034
behaves with water.

49
00:02:23,284 --> 00:02:26,364
Though in that same
study, in punishing dryness,

50
00:02:26,424 --> 00:02:30,437
below five humidity, one stress
measure actually got worse.

51
00:02:30,437 --> 00:02:31,837
It is not a force field.

52
00:02:31,987 --> 00:02:35,327
It is better understood as a
supporting ingredient that may help

53
00:02:35,577 --> 00:02:39,007
skin handle dehydration and
environmental stress more comfortably.

54
00:02:39,517 --> 00:02:42,730
So does it do
anything to actual people?

55
00:02:42,790 --> 00:02:44,970
In one randomised, double blind study,

56
00:02:44,970 --> 00:02:49,904
a women used a cream with
two ectoin against the same

57
00:02:49,904 --> 00:02:51,014
cream without it.

58
00:02:51,314 --> 00:02:52,926
On the study's own measure,

59
00:02:52,926 --> 00:02:56,639
the women themselves preferred the ectoin
version. That is a real result.

60
00:02:56,639 --> 00:02:59,059
And it is a preference, not
a machine reading their skin.

61
00:02:59,209 --> 00:03:03,589
A second randomised study followed sixty
people with mild to moderate

62
00:03:03,669 --> 00:03:05,289
atopic dermatitis, eczema.

63
00:03:05,289 --> 00:03:08,881
Over four weeks, an ectoin cream
performed about as well as

64
00:03:08,881 --> 00:03:11,926
the barrier cream it was tested
against, and was well tolerated.

65
00:03:12,176 --> 00:03:13,176
Encouraging.

66
00:03:13,176 --> 00:03:15,366
Not proof that ectoin eczema,

67
00:03:15,366 --> 00:03:19,116
reverses ageing, or replaces
anything a dermatologist prescribes.

68
00:03:19,366 --> 00:03:22,043
And the evidence base
is genuinely small.

69
00:03:22,043 --> 00:03:26,701
Search PubMed today and you will
find twelve ectoin clinical trials. Twelve.

70
00:03:26,981 --> 00:03:31,041
Some of that research also comes
from people who sell the ingredient.

71
00:03:31,221 --> 00:03:33,136
Bitop, Merck, Kao. So.

72
00:03:33,581 --> 00:03:35,672
Promising supporting ingredient, yes.

73
00:03:35,672 --> 00:03:37,892
Miracle molecule, no.

74
00:03:38,432 --> 00:03:42,102
Ectoin is most interesting if
your skin runs dry,

75
00:03:42,242 --> 00:03:46,326
sensitive, over cleansed, or irritated
by a strong routine.

76
00:03:46,416 --> 00:03:48,796
It sits comfortably alongside panthenol,

77
00:03:48,796 --> 00:03:50,946
glycerin, squalane and ceramides.

78
00:03:51,196 --> 00:03:54,585
But do not buy it off the
front of the bottle. Turn it around.

79
00:03:54,715 --> 00:03:56,605
Plenty of brands do
print a number.

80
00:03:56,735 --> 00:04:00,305
Paula's Choice says seven
The Ordinary says two.

81
00:04:00,595 --> 00:04:04,723
And Abib's Ectoin Panthenol eleven That
eleven is the two of

82
00:04:04,723 --> 00:04:05,743
them added together.

83
00:04:05,972 --> 00:04:08,446
On the ingredient list,
panthenol is second.

84
00:04:08,446 --> 00:04:09,628
Ectoin is eleventh.

85
00:04:09,628 --> 00:04:12,319
The big number on the front
is not always the ectoin number.

86
00:04:12,469 --> 00:04:16,188
The rest is judgement, not evidence.

87
00:04:16,188 --> 00:04:18,325
Is it fragrance if
fragrance bothers you?

88
00:04:18,617 --> 00:04:21,081
Does the formula carry
other useful moisturisers?

89
00:04:21,081 --> 00:04:24,643
One good ingredient cannot rescue
a badly built product.

90
00:04:25,143 --> 00:04:27,531
K did not invent ectoin.

91
00:04:27,781 --> 00:04:31,111
What Korean formulators are doing well
is pairing it with barrier

92
00:04:31,111 --> 00:04:33,291
ingredients in light, wearable textures.

93
00:04:33,541 --> 00:04:37,131
That fits the wider move away
from aggressive transformation and towards

94
00:04:37,211 --> 00:04:38,951
skin that simply stays comfortable.

95
00:04:38,951 --> 00:04:41,498
You will already find it
in Korean barrier serums,

96
00:04:41,608 --> 00:04:45,568
toners and sun products, often in
smaller type than the trend suggests.

97
00:04:46,168 --> 00:04:49,171
So next time you see
ectoin on an ingredient list,

98
00:04:49,171 --> 00:04:50,630
remember what you are looking at.

99
00:04:50,630 --> 00:04:53,345
A survival strategy, borrowed from bacteria.

100
00:04:53,345 --> 00:04:55,305
Not the new hyaluronic acid.

101
00:04:55,305 --> 00:04:56,427
Not a miracle.

102
00:04:56,427 --> 00:04:58,642
A genuinely interesting supporting molecule.

103
00:04:59,142 --> 00:05:00,970
Here is the one
thing worth doing.

104
00:05:01,190 --> 00:05:04,532
Turn the bottle around and find
the actual percentage before you buy.

105
00:05:04,532 --> 00:05:08,569
And tell me, would you put a
bacteria survival molecule on your face?
